<!-- Hero -->
<header class="mb-12">
  <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
    <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-violet" />
    <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-violet">Core Concepts</span>
  </span>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    Parameters
  </h1>
  <p class="text-base text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose">
    Parameters are the model's memories — the billions of numbers that encode everything
    it has learned. Adjust the slider below to see how model size shapes what's possible.
  </p>
</header>

<!-- Section: What are parameters? -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    What are parameters?
  </h2>
  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4">
    <p>
      A parameter is a single number inside the model — a weight or a bias — that gets
      adjusted during training. Together, these numbers form the model's entire "knowledge."
      When you ask a model a question, it's these parameters doing all the work: transforming
      your input tokens through layers of mathematical operations to produce a response.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think of parameters like the synaptic connections in a brain. Each connection has a
      strength — a number that determines how strongly one neuron influences another. A human
      brain has roughly 100 trillion synapses. A modern large language model might have
      70 billion parameters. The model's intelligence emerges from the pattern of all these
      numbers working together.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two types of parameters in a neural network layer:
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
        Weights
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        The main parameters. Each neuron has a weight for every input it receives.
        In a layer with 4096 inputs and 4096 outputs, that's 16.7 million weights.
      </p>
      <div class="p-3 rounded-md bg-gray-50 font-mono text-xs text-blue-violet">
        weights = inputs × outputs
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
        Biases
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        An offset added to each neuron's output. One bias per output neuron — much
        fewer than weights, but essential for shifting the activation threshold.
      </p>
      <div class="p-3 rounded-md bg-gray-50 font-mono text-blue-violet">
        biases = outputs
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Interactive visualization -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-2">
    Model anatomy
  </h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-6">
    Explore how parameters are distributed across a model's layers. Click any layer to
    see its parameter breakdown. Use the slider to scale from a tiny model to a large one
    and watch the parameter counts explode.
  </p>
  <ModelAnatomy containerClass="mb-6" />
</section>

<!-- Section: How parameters get their values -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    How parameters get their values: learning from mistakes
  </h2>
  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4">
    <p>
      When a model is first created, every parameter is set to a small random number.
      The model knows nothing — it's like a brain before any learning has happened.
      Training is the process of adjusting all these numbers until the model produces
      good outputs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The adjustment happens through <strong>gradient descent</strong> — an algorithm
      that's essentially "learning from mistakes." Here's how it works:
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-4 mt-6">
    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-blue-violet text-white text-xs font-bold">
          1
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">
          Make a prediction
        </h3>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
        Feed input through the network. Each layer transforms the data using its current
        parameters. The final layer produces a prediction — like guessing the next word
        in a sentence.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-blue-violet text-white text-xs font-bold">
          2
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">
          Measure the mistake
        </h3>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
        Compare the prediction to the correct answer. The <strong>loss function</strong>
        quantifies how wrong the model was — a single number. Higher loss means a bigger
        mistake. This is the model's feedback signal.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-blue-violet text-white text-xs font-bold">
          3
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">
          Calculate the gradient
        </h3>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
        For every single parameter, compute how much it contributed to the error.
        This is called the <strong>gradient</strong> — it tells you which direction to
        adjust each parameter to reduce the loss. Backpropagation is the algorithm that
        efficiently computes these gradients through the entire network.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-blue-violet text-white text-xs font-bold">
          4
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">
          Update the parameters
        </h3>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
        Nudge each parameter in the direction that reduces error. The <strong>learning
        rate</strong> controls how big each nudge is — too small and learning is slow;
        too large and the model overshoots and diverges. Then repeat from step 1,
        millions of times, over thousands of training examples.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose mt-6 space-y-4">
    <p>
      After training on hundreds of billions of tokens, each parameter has settled into
      a value that represents something the model learned: a grammar rule, a fact, a
      reasoning pattern, or a piece of world knowledge. The model's capability is
      entirely determined by these parameter values.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section: The scaling story -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    The scaling story: more parameters, more capability
  </h2>
  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4 mb-6">
    <p>
      The most consistent finding in modern AI research: bigger models are smarter.
      More parameters mean the model can store more knowledge, learn more complex
      patterns, and handle more nuanced tasks. But the relationship isn't linear —
      capability tends to grow as a power law with model size.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-gray-200">
          <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Model</th>
          <th class="text-right py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Parameters</th>
          <th class="text-right py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Disk (FP16)</th>
          <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Era</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
        <tr class="bg-white">
          <td class="py-2 px-3 font-medium text-near-black">GPT-2</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-blue-violet">1.5B</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-gray-500">3 GB</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-500">2019</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="bg-gray-50">
          <td class="py-2 px-3 font-medium text-near-black">GPT-3</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-blue-violet">175B</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-gray-500">350 GB</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-500">2020</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="bg-white">
          <td class="py-2 px-3 font-medium text-near-black">LLaMA 2</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-blue-violet">70B</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-gray-500">140 GB</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-500">2023</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="bg-gray-50">
          <td class="py-2 px-3 font-medium text-near-black">GPT-4</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-blue-violet">~1.8T</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right font-mono text-gray-500">~3.6 TB</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-500">2023</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4">
    <p>
      But parameters alone aren't enough. The scaling laws show that you need to scale
      three things together: <strong>model size</strong> (parameters), <strong>data size</strong>
      (training tokens), and <strong>compute</strong> (the training run itself). Double the
      parameters without more data, and the model overfits. Double the data without more
      parameters, and the model can't retain what it learns.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why the largest models require thousands of GPUs running for weeks. Training
      a 70B parameter model on 2 trillion tokens costs millions of dollars in compute alone.
      But the capability gains — from writing coherent essays to passing professional exams
      to generating working code — justify the investment.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section: The tradeoffs -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    The tradeoffs of scale
  </h2>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #5E5CE6; background-color: #5E5CE612;">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <rect x="2" y="2" width="20" height="20" rx="2" />
          <path d="M12 8v8M8 12h8" />
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Inference cost
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Every time a model generates text, it computes through all its parameters.
          A 70B model requires roughly 140 billion multiply-add operations per token
          generated (forward pass through all layers). This means bigger models are
          slower and more expensive to run — a key reason smaller models remain
          practical for many applications.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #7B79D6; background-color: #7B79D612;">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z" />
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Environmental impact
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Training a large model consumes megawatt-hours of electricity — comparable
          to the energy usage of hundreds of households over a year. The carbon footprint
          of training GPT-3 was estimated at 500+ tons of CO₂. This has sparked debate
          about whether the relentless pursuit of scale is sustainable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #9896C6; background-color: #9896C612;">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
          <path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01" />
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Diminishing returns
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          The power-law scaling means each doubling of parameters gives smaller gains
          than the last. Going from 1B to 2B params might double capability, but going
          from 100B to 200B might only improve it by 40%. Some researchers argue we're
          approaching the limits of what scale alone can deliver.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Key takeaways -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    Key takeaways
  </h2>
  <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6">
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>Parameters are the model's memories — the numbers that encode everything it learned</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>Weights connect neurons; biases shift activations. Together they define each layer's transformation</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>Gradient descent adjusts parameters by learning from mistakes — the core training algorithm</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>The embedding and output layers hold the most parameters in typical architectures</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>More parameters generally means more capability, but with growing cost and tradeoffs</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

